#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069937 :tutorial/content "A radical expression like $\\sqrt{{\\color{black}x}}$ can also be written using a fractional exponent as $x^{1/2}$. They mean the same thing.\n\nSimilarly,\n\n- a cube root such as $\\sqrt[{\\color{blue}3\\!\\phantom{p}}]{x}$ is equivalent to $x^{1/\\color{blue}{3}}$,\n- a tenth root $\\sqrt[{\\color{blue}10\\!\\phantom{p}}]{x}$ is equivalent to $x^{1/\\color{blue}{10}}$,\n\nand so on. In general, we have the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a261c9ab-28f0-59c3-ae99-985dc03258d0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1475 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
